NNAAMMEE
     ggppiiooiiiicc — GPIO I2C bit-banging device driver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee ggppiioo
           ddeevviiccee ggppiiooiiiicc
           ddeevviiccee iiiiccbbbb
           ddeevviiccee iiiiccbbuuss

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following line in loader.conf(5):

           gpioiic_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggppiiooiiiicc driver provides an IIC bit-banging interface using two GPIO
     pins for the SCL and SDA lines on the bus.

     ggppiiooiiiicc simulates an open collector kind of output when managing the pins
     on the bus, even on systems which don't directly support configuring gpio
     pins in that mode.  The pins are never driven to the logical value of
     '1'.  They are driven to '0' or switched to input mode (Hi-Z/tri-state),
     and an external pullup resistor pulls the line to the 1 state unless some
     other device on the bus is driving it to 0.

HHIINNTTSS CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN
     On a device.hints(5) based system, such as MIPS, these values are config‐
     urable for ggppiiooiiiicc:

     _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._a_t     The ggppiioobbuuss you are attaching to.  Normally just
                            gpiobus0 on systems with a single bank of gpio
                            pins.

     _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._p_i_n_s   This is a bitmask of the pins on the ggppiioobbuuss that
                            are to be used for SCLOCK and SDATA from the GPIO
                            IIC bit-banging bus.  To configure pin 0 and 7,
                            use the bitmask of 0b10000001 and convert it to a
                            hexadecimal value of 0x0081.  Please note that
                            this mask should only ever have two bits set (any
                            other bits - i.e., pins - will be ignored).  Be‐
                            cause ggppiiooiiiicc must be a child of the gpiobus, both
                            gpio pins must be part of that bus.

     _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._s_c_l    Indicates which bit in the _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._p_i_n_s
                            should be used as the SCLOCK source.  Optional,
                            defaults to 0.

     _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._s_d_a    Indicates which bit in the _h_i_n_t_._g_p_i_o_i_i_c_._%_d_._p_i_n_s
                            should be used as the SDATA source.  Optional, de‐
                            faults to 1.

FFDDTT CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN
     On an FDT(4) based system, such as ARM, the DTS node for ggppiiooiiiicc conforms
     to the standard bindings document i2c/i2c-gpio.yaml.  The device node
     typically appears at the root of the device tree.  The following is an
     example of a ggppiiooiiiicc node with one slave device on the IIC bus:

     / {
             gpioiic0 {
                     compatible = "i2c-gpio";
                     pinctrl-names = "default";
                     pinctrl-0 = <&pinctrl_gpioiic0>;
                     scl-gpios = <&gpio1  5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
                     sda-gpios = <&gpio7 11 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
                     status = "okay";

                     /* One slave device on the i2c bus. */
                     rtc@51 {
                             compatible="nxp,pcf2127";
                             reg = <0x51>;
                             status = "okay";
                     };
             };
     };

     Where:

     _c_o_m_p_a_t_i_b_l_e  Should be set to "i2c-gpio".  The deprecated string "gpioiic"
                 is also accepted for backwards compatibility.

     _s_c_l_-_g_p_i_o_s _s_d_a_-_g_p_i_o_s
                 These properties indicate which GPIO pins should be used for
                 clock and data on the GPIO IIC bit-banging bus.  There is no
                 requirement that the two pins belong to the same gpio con‐
                 troller.

     _p_i_n_c_t_r_l_-_n_a_m_e_s _p_i_n_c_t_r_l_-_0
                 These properties may be required to configure the chosen pins
                 as gpio pins, unless the pins default to that state on your
                 system.

